    "photoUrl": "/practitioner_guide/photos/PG_Page_078_WalkingYourWhy_IMG_5690.JPG",
    "lineCount": 27,
    "rawText": "Invitation→LearningLab What was the“function”of the emotion?Whatvalue or need was it signposting? What the Func? When we ask“what the func?”we internalize emotions as guides instead of threats.This insight is freeing.It gives us more flexibility,more self- compassion,and more clarity about the values or needs thatmatter.It helps us to adapt. This isn't just for individuals.Teams and organizations can ask the same question:What'sthefunction ofthisfrustration,thisexcitement,thisfatigue? Often,emotions atwork are collective signpostspointing to values or needs that are askingfor attention,like respect,recognition,or balance. PocketPractice Use\"What In2minutes or less the Func?\" You can use WTF? in any situation: alone,in conversation with others, Anywhere or with your whole team. Here'show: 1.Notice the emotion. Pause to name what's being felt. 2.Ask:What the func? What need or value might this emotion be signaling?For example,frustration might signal a need for fairness or clarity. 3.Respond with wisdom.Instead of reacting to the emotion,take (or invite) one small action that honors the underlying value. ShowUp Step Out Walk Your Why Move On 78 @2026SUSANDAVID·EMOTIONALAGILITY@·VERSION2.0",
    "htmlContent": "<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">Invitation→LearningLab What was the“function”of the emotion?Whatvalue or need was</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">it signposting?</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">What the Func?</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">When we ask“what the func?”we internalize emotions as guides instead</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">of threats.This insight is freeing.It gives us more flexibility,more self-</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">compassion,and more clarity about the values or needs thatmatter.It helps us</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">to adapt.</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">This isn't just for individuals.Teams and organizations can ask the same</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">question:What'sthefunction ofthisfrustration,thisexcitement,thisfatigue?</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">Often,emotions atwork are collective signpostspointing to values or needs that</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">are askingfor attention,like respect,recognition,or balance.</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">PocketPractice</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">Use&quot;What In2minutes or less</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">the Func?&quot; You can use WTF? in any situation: alone,in conversation with others,</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">Anywhere or with your whole team.</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">Here'show:</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">1.Notice the emotion. Pause to name what's being felt.</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">2.Ask:What the func? What need or value might this emotion</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">be signaling?For example,frustration might signal a need for</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">fairness or clarity.</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">3.Respond with wisdom.Instead of reacting to the emotion,take (or</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">invite) one small action that honors the underlying value.</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">ShowUp</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">Step Out</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">Walk Your Why</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">Move On</p>\n<p class=\"guide-paragraph\">78 @2026SUSANDAVID·EMOTIONALAGILITY@·VERSION2.0</p>"
