    "lineCount": 34,
    "rawText": "FieldNotes→CaseStudy Scenario: You're a team leader with a major project deadline tomorrow. What Values You value both honesty and kindness.A team member's work is subpar. You need to decidewhether to give tough feedbacktonight orlet it slide Are (And Aren't) until afterthe deadline. Goal: Ship the release by tomorrow 5 pm with zero priority1 defects. Values:Honesty and kindness. Purpose:To build trust,learning,and long-term team growth. Principle:As a team,we deliver candid feedback within 24 hours, privately and with care,even under deadline pressure. When we sort these terms clearly,values emerge as thecompass.Goals changuoseenrincilguidbualusint hy Goals are destinations.You can cross them off a list. Values are directions.Qualities of action that guide how you show up. ·Purpose is the bigger“why.” The larger aim that deepens over time. Principles are the ground rules you commit to.The bridge between values and goals, especially when making tradeoffs. When two good options appear to honor your values,the next guiding question becomes:Which choice better advances your purpose? If the pupose is longterm trust and growth,offring timely,caring feedbackbefore the deadline maybest serve it,even undergoal pressure. The principle supports this choice by giving a shared standard for how to act — one that reduces decision fatigue and anchors integrity. This ishowgoals,values,urpose,andrinciplesworktogether support courageous and aligned action,especially when stakes are high orchoices are complex. As an example: Imagine a nurse working in a busy hospital. Their goal might be to help a patient recover from surgery. Their values of compassion and diligence shape how they show up in every interaction,even on the hardest days. Their purpose might be to ease suffering and promote healing. we choose safety and escalate concerns immediately. WALKINGYOURWHY 81",
